From 5af2801182d493352e990f5031da9eac6b3f9f71 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "David C. Manuelda" <StormByte@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 15:19:32 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] avformat/movenc: preserve long gaps between timed-text
 subtitles

check_pkt() rejects DTS deltas greater than or equal to INT_MAX and
replaces the incoming DTS with the previous sample's DTS plus one,
discarding its PTS. With a 1/1000000 subtitle time base, an empty interval
of 2147.483647 seconds is sufficient to trigger this recovery path.
Sparse mov_text subtitles can therefore appear thousands of seconds
before their intended presentation time, even when the incoming packets
have valid, monotonically increasing timestamps.

The empty sample inserted at the end of a cue does not prevent this:
its duration is determined by the next sample, which may be hours later.
Relaxing check_pkt() alone is not sufficient because the sample-duration
helpers also require signed 32-bit durations.

Split long empty intervals by inserting additional two-byte empty
mov_text samples at steps of INT_MAX - 1 track ticks. This keeps every
generated sample duration below the existing validation limit while
preserving cue timestamps, durations, text and the original timescale.
Apply the same handling to leading empty intervals.

Rescale the triggering packet's DTS into each subtitle track's time base
before comparing it with that track's duration. Packets from other tracks
may use different time bases and must not be compared as raw integers.
Ignore packets without a DTS for this subtitle-end insertion pass.

A minimal reproducer uses an SRT file containing:

  1
  00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,000
  First cue

  2
  01:53:17,916 --> 01:53:26,425
  Second cue

Encode with:

  ffmpeg -i sparse.srt -c:s mov_text sparse.mp4
  ffmpeg -i sparse.mp4 -map 0:s:0 -c:s srt roundtrip.srt

Before this change the second cue starts at approximately 2.000001
seconds instead of 6797.916 seconds. After this change both cues retain
their intended start times and durations.

Verified with signed-duration boundary cases, gaps requiring multiple
empty samples, mixed track time bases and fragmented MP4 output.
---
 libavformat/movenc.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libavformat/movenc.c b/libavformat/movenc.c
index 6f93eb3e36..4aea51737e 100644
--- a/libavformat/movenc.c
+++ b/libavformat/movenc.c
@@ -7643,15 +7643,31 @@ static int mov_write_packet(AVFormatContext *s, AVPacket *pkt)
          *
          * 3) See mov_write_trailer for how the final end sample is
          * handled.
+         *
+         * 4) Split long empty intervals before check_pkt's signed duration
+         * limit. At a microsecond timescale, sparse subtitles can exceed
+         * this limit after only about 36 minutes without a new cue.
          */
         for (i = 0; i < mov->nb_tracks; i++) {
             MOVTrack *trk = &mov->tracks[i];
+            int64_t dts, end;
             int ret;
 
-            if (trk->par->codec_id == AV_CODEC_ID_MOV_TEXT &&
-                trk->track_duration < pkt->dts &&
-                (trk->entry == 0 || !trk->last_sample_is_subtitle_end)) {
-                ret = mov_write_subtitle_end_packet(s, i, trk->track_duration);
+            if (trk->par->codec_id != AV_CODEC_ID_MOV_TEXT ||
+                pkt->dts == AV_NOPTS_VALUE)
+                continue;
+
+            dts = av_rescale_q(pkt->dts, s->streams[pkt->stream_index]->time_base,
+                              (AVRational){1, trk->timescale});
+            while (trk->track_duration < dts) {
+                if (trk->entry == 0 || !trk->last_sample_is_subtitle_end)
+                    end = trk->track_duration;
+                else if (dts - trk->track_duration >= INT_MAX)
+                    end = trk->track_duration + INT_MAX - 1;
+                else
+                    break;
+
+                ret = mov_write_subtitle_end_packet(s, i, end);
                 if (ret < 0) return ret;
                 trk->last_sample_is_subtitle_end = 1;
             }
